/* Verse Games: THE WHOLE COUNSEL · Nehemiah chapters 6-10
   Verse text is filled/verified from the KJV by tools/fill-verses.js */
VG.registerBibleChapters("nehemiah", [
{n:6, name:"A Great Work", seal:{ref:"Nehemiah 6:3",verse:"And I sent messengers unto them, saying, I am doing a great work, so that I cannot come down: why should the work cease, whilst I leave it, and come down to you?"}, facts:["Did you know Sanballat's fifth message came as an 'open letter,' so anyone could read its charges of rebellion? It was an ancient smear campaign, and Nehemiah answered it in one sentence, then went back to building.","Did you know Shemaiah's 'prophecy' urged Nehemiah to hide inside the temple, where only priests could lawfully go? Nehemiah spotted the trap, because a true word from God never asks you to break God's law.","Did you know the wall was finished in fifty-two days, so quickly that even Israel's enemies confessed 'this work was wrought of our God'? When God is in the work, the result preaches for itself."], questions:[
{q:"What news had reached Sanballat, Tobiah, and Geshem the Arabian?", options:["That the wall was builded and no breach was left in it","That Nehemiah had fled back to Persia","That the gates of Jerusalem were burned again","That the king had sent an army to Judah"], a:0, ref:"Nehemiah 6:1",verse:"Now it came to pass, when Sanballat, and Tobiah, and Geshem the Arabian, and the rest of our enemies, heard that I had builded the wall, and that there was no breach left therein; (though at that time I had not set up the doors upon the gates;)", insight:"The wall stood complete, and the news traveled fast. Finished work has a way of announcing itself before you say a word."},
{q:"Where did Sanballat and Geshem invite Nehemiah to meet them?", options:["In some village in the plain of Ono","In the king's palace at Susa","In the temple at Jerusalem","In the valley of Rephaim"], a:0, ref:"Nehemiah 6:2",verse:"That Sanballat and Geshem sent unto me, saying, Come, let us meet together in some one of the villages in the plain of Ono. But they thought to do me mischief.", insight:"They chose neutral ground and friendly words, but Nehemiah saw through it: 'they thought to do me mischief.' A godly leader weighs the invitation, not just the tone."},
{q:"What reply did Nehemiah send to their invitation?", options:["I am doing a great work, so that I cannot come down","I will come down after the sabbath","Bring the king's seal and I will obey","I am too weary to travel"], a:0, ref:"Nehemiah 6:3",verse:"And I sent messengers unto them, saying, I am doing a great work, so that I cannot come down: why should the work cease, whilst I leave it, and come down to you?", insight:"Nehemiah measured the invitation against the work God gave him, and the work won. Every distraction deserves that same honest measurement."},
{q:"How many times did they repeat the invitation in this same way?", options:["Four times","Two times","Seven times","Ten times"], a:0, ref:"Nehemiah 6:4",verse:"Yet they sent unto me four times after this sort; and I answered them after the same manner.", insight:"Four times they tried, and four times he answered the same way. Steadfastness is simply saying no as many times as it takes."},
{q:"On the fifth approach, what made the message different?", options:["It came as an open letter anyone could read","It was sealed with the king's ring","It arrived by night","It was written in Persian"], a:0, ref:"Nehemiah 6:5",verse:"Then sent Sanballat his servant unto me in like manner the fifth time with an open letter in his hand;", insight:"An open letter was a public smear, meant to pressure Nehemiah through shame. He answered slander with truth and kept building, which is still the right reply."},
{q:"What did the letter, quoting Gashmu, charge Nehemiah with planning?", options:["To rebel, and to make himself king of the Jews","To burn the temple","To bribe the king's officers","To lead the people back to Babylon"], a:0, ref:"Nehemiah 6:6",verse:"Wherein was written, It is reported among the heathen, and Gashmu saith it, that thou and the Jews think to rebel: for which cause thou buildest the wall, that thou mayest be their king, according to these words.", insight:"The charge was invented, but it sounded official because 'Gashmu saith it.' A rumor with a name attached is still a rumor; Nehemiah refused to answer fiction as if it were fact."},
{q:"According to the letter, what were the appointed prophets supposedly preaching in Jerusalem?", options:["There is a king in Judah","The wall must be torn down","The sabbath is no more","Sanballat is governor"], a:0, ref:"Nehemiah 6:7",verse:"And thou hast also appointed prophets to preach of thee at Jerusalem, saying, There is a king in Judah: and now shall it be reported to the king according to these words. Come now therefore, and let us take counsel together.", insight:"They forged a prophecy to frighten him into a meeting. When a 'word from God' serves your enemies' agenda, it is worth testing before you tremble."},
{q:"How did Nehemiah answer these charges?", options:["There are no such things done as thou sayest, but thou feignest them out of thine own heart","I will clear my name before the king","The prophets spoke without my knowledge","I confess it all and ask mercy"], a:0, ref:"Nehemiah 6:8",verse:"Then I sent unto him, saying, There are no such things done as thou sayest, but thou feignest them out of thine own heart.", insight:"One plain sentence dismantled the whole letter. Lies lose their power the moment someone calmly names them as lies."},
{q:"What did Nehemiah pray when the threats aimed to weaken the workers' hands?", options:["Now therefore, O God, strengthen my hands","O God, destroy Sanballat","Lord, send fire from heaven","Hide me from my enemies"], a:0, ref:"Nehemiah 6:9",verse:"For they all made us afraid, saying, Their hands shall be weakened from the work, that it be not done. Now therefore, O God, strengthen my hands.", insight:"He did not pray for revenge; he prayed for strength to keep working. When opposition comes, ask God for stronger hands before you ask Him for fewer enemies."},
{q:"Whose house did Nehemiah visit, finding the man 'shut up' inside?", options:["Shemaiah the son of Delaiah","Tobiah the Ammonite","Eliashib the priest","Hanani his brother"], a:0, ref:"Nehemiah 6:10",verse:"Afterward I came unto the house of Shemaiah the son of Delaiah the son of Mehetabeel, who was shut up; and he said, Let us meet together in the house of God, within the temple, and let us shut the doors of the temple: for they will come to slay thee; yea, in the night will they come to slay thee.", insight:"Shemaiah played the fearful prophet, shut in his house with an urgent warning. Nehemiah went to listen, which shows discernment starts with paying attention, not with panic."},
{q:"What did Shemaiah urge Nehemiah to do?", options:["Meet in the house of God, within the temple, and shut the doors","Flee to Egypt that very night","Raise an army at Mizpah","Write to the king for soldiers"], a:0, ref:"Nehemiah 6:10",verse:"Afterward I came unto the house of Shemaiah the son of Delaiah the son of Mehetabeel, who was shut up; and he said, Let us meet together in the house of God, within the temple, and let us shut the doors of the temple: for they will come to slay thee; yea, in the night will they come to slay thee.", insight:"It sounded like safety, but only priests could lawfully enter the temple. God's true counsel never asks you to break God's law to save your life."},
{q:"How did Nehemiah answer him?", options:["Should such a man as I flee? I will not go in","I will go, but only with the priests","Meet me at the water gate instead","Give me one night to decide"], a:0, ref:"Nehemiah 6:11",verse:"And I said, Should such a man as I flee? and who is there, that, being as I am, would go into the temple to save his life? I will not go in.", insight:"'Should such a man as I flee?' His identity settled the decision. When you know who you are before God, fear has to argue with something stronger than feelings."},
{q:"What did Nehemiah perceive about Shemaiah's prophecy?", options:["That God had not sent him, but Tobiah and Sanballat had hired him","That it was a true word from the LORD","That it came from the prophetess Noadiah","That it was only a dream"], a:0, ref:"Nehemiah 6:12",verse:"And, lo, I perceived that God had not sent him; but that he pronounced this prophecy against me: for Tobiah and Sanballat had hired him.", insight:"Nehemiah tested the prophecy and found the payroll behind it. A message that flatters your fear and serves your enemies deserves a second look."},
{q:"Why had Shemaiah been hired?", options:["That Nehemiah should be afraid, and do so, and sin, giving them matter for an evil report","To repair the temple doors","To carry letters to the king","To teach the law to the people"], a:0, ref:"Nehemiah 6:13",verse:"Therefore was he hired, that I should be afraid, and do so, and sin, and that they might have matter for an evil report, that they might reproach me.", insight:"The goal was never the temple meeting; it was a ruined reputation. The enemy often aims at your name when he cannot stop your work."},
{q:"Which prophetess does Nehemiah name in his prayer about those who tried to frighten him?", options:["Noadiah","Huldah","Miriam","Deborah"], a:0, ref:"Nehemiah 6:14",verse:"My God, think thou upon Tobiah and Sanballat according to these their works, and on the prophetess Noadiah, and the rest of the prophets, that would have put me in fear.", insight:"Nehemiah hands even the prophets who opposed him over to God: 'My God, think thou upon them.' Some battles are won by prayer, not by confrontation."},
{q:"On what day was the wall finished?", options:["The twenty-fifth day of the month Elul","The first day of the seventh month","The tenth day of the month","The last day of the year"], a:0, ref:"Nehemiah 6:15",verse:"So the wall was finished in the twenty and fifth day of the month Elul, in fifty and two days.", insight:"The date is recorded because the day mattered. God keeps a calendar of finished work, and He remembers what His people complete."},
{q:"How long had the whole work taken?", options:["Fifty and two days","One hundred days","Seven months","A full year"], a:0, ref:"Nehemiah 6:15",verse:"So the wall was finished in the twenty and fifth day of the month Elul, in fifty and two days.", insight:"Fifty-two days for what had lain in ruins for generations. When God moves and His people obey, decades of decay can be undone in weeks."},
{q:"When the surrounding nations saw the finished wall, what did they perceive?", options:["That this work was wrought of our God","That Nehemiah had hired foreign soldiers","That the king of Persia had sent an army","That the wall would soon fall again"], a:0, ref:"Nehemiah 6:16",verse:"And it came to pass, that when all our enemies heard thereof, and all the heathen that were about us saw these things, they were much cast down in their own eyes: for they perceived that this work was wrought of our God.", insight:"Even the enemies read the signature on the work. Excellence done for God becomes its own testimony to those watching."},
{q:"In those days, what were the nobles of Judah doing?", options:["Sending many letters to Tobiah, and receiving his letters","Building houses inside Jerusalem","Fasting and praying for the wall","Gathering silver for the king's tax"], a:0, ref:"Nehemiah 6:17",verse:"Moreover in those days the nobles of Judah sent many letters unto Tobiah, and the letters of Tobiah came unto them.", insight:"While Nehemiah fought Tobiah, Judah's nobles were corresponding with him. The hardest opposition often comes from inside your own camp."},
{q:"Why were many in Judah sworn to Tobiah?", options:["Because he was the son in law of Shechaniah the son of Arah","Because he was the king's cupbearer","Because he had lent them silver","Because he was a priest of the LORD"], a:0, ref:"Nehemiah 6:18",verse:"For there were many in Judah sworn unto him, because he was the son in law of Shechaniah the son of Arah; and his son Johanan had taken the daughter of Meshullam the son of Berechiah.", insight:"Marriage ties had made Tobiah family to Judah's nobles, and family ties can blind judgment. Nehemiah saw the alliance for what it was, no matter whose relative it involved."}
]},
{n:7, name:"The Numbered People", seal:{ref:"Nehemiah 7:66",verse:"The whole congregation together was forty and two thousand three hundred and threescore,"}, facts:["Did you know the register Nehemiah found listed the families of the first return from Babylon, preserved for decades? God keeps the family records even when His people forget them.","Did you know priests who could not prove their lineage were barred from the most holy things until a priest with Urim and Thummim could decide? The priesthood was guarded too carefully to admit anyone on his own word.","Did you know the whole congregation numbered 42,360, and that Hanani, the brother who first carried the bad news to Nehemiah, was put in charge of Jerusalem? Faithful messengers become trusted stewards."], questions:[
{q:"When the wall was built and the doors set up, which three groups were appointed?", options:["The porters, the singers, and the Levites","The priests, the scribes, and the elders","The captains, the horsemen, and the archers","The merchants, the builders, and the farmers"], a:0, ref:"Nehemiah 7:1",verse:"Now it came to pass, when the wall was built, and I had set up the doors, and the porters and the singers and the Levites were appointed,", insight:"Worship and watchfulness were organized before anything else. A finished wall still needs gatekeepers and singers; protection and praise go together."},
{q:"To whom did Nehemiah give charge over Jerusalem?", options:["His brother Hanani, and Hananiah the ruler of the palace","Ezra the scribe and Zerubbabel","Sanballat and Tobiah","The elders of Judah"], a:0, ref:"Nehemiah 7:2",verse:"That I gave my brother Hanani, and Hananiah the ruler of the palace, charge over Jerusalem: for he was a faithful man, and feared God above many.", insight:"Hanani was the brother who first brought Nehemiah the bad news from Jerusalem. Faithful messengers often become faithful stewards."},
{q:"What is said of Hananiah in this verse?", options:["He was a faithful man, and feared God above many","He was the richest man in Jerusalem","He was a mighty man of war","He was the king's cupbearer"], a:0, ref:"Nehemiah 7:2",verse:"That I gave my brother Hanani, and Hananiah the ruler of the palace, charge over Jerusalem: for he was a faithful man, and feared God above many.", insight:"His qualification was character: faithful, and God-fearing above many. Nehemiah chose leaders by devotion, not by resume."},
{q:"When were the gates of Jerusalem to be opened?", options:["Not until the sun be hot","At the crowing of the cock","Only on the sabbath","When the singers began"], a:0, ref:"Nehemiah 7:3",verse:"And I said unto them, Let not the gates of Jerusalem be opened until the sun be hot; and while they stand by, let them shut the doors, and bar them: and appoint watches of the inhabitants of Jerusalem, every one in his watch, and every one to be over against his house.", insight:"Late opening and barred doors were Nehemiah's security policy. Wisdom locks the gate; presumption leaves it open."},
{q:"What was the city's problem after the wall was done?", options:["The city was large and great, but the people were few and the houses were not builded","A plague had emptied the streets","The king had ordered an evacuation","The walls had fallen again"], a:0, ref:"Nehemiah 7:4",verse:"Now the city was large and great: but the people were few therein, and the houses were not builded.", insight:"Walls without people are just scenery. Nehemiah saw that a secure city still needs citizens, so he set out to fill it."},
{q:"What did God put into Nehemiah's heart to do?", options:["To gather the nobles, rulers, and people to be reckoned by genealogy","To build a second wall","To march against Samaria","To number the army for war"], a:0, ref:"Nehemiah 7:5",verse:"And my God put into mine heart to gather together the nobles, and the rulers, and the people, that they might be reckoned by genealogy. And I found a register of the genealogy of them which came up at the first, and found written therein,", insight:"The census was God's idea, planted in Nehemiah's heart. Good administration can be as spiritual as prayer when God puts it there."},
{q:"How many were the children of Parosh?", options:["2,172","2,127","2,712","1,272"], a:0, ref:"Nehemiah 7:8",verse:"The children of Parosh, two thousand an hundred seventy and two.", insight:"Every family is counted by name and number. God keeps careful records of His people; nobody is a rounding error to Him."},
{q:"How many were the children of Senaah?", options:["3,930","3,903","3,390","2,930"], a:0, ref:"Nehemiah 7:38",verse:"The children of Senaah, three thousand nine hundred and thirty.", insight:"Senaah's 3,930 was the largest family group in the register. God grows some families large, and every one of them is written down."},
{q:"How many Levites returned?", options:["74","47","148","138"], a:0, ref:"Nehemiah 7:43",verse:"The Levites: the children of Jeshua, of Kadmiel, and of the children of Hodevah, seventy and four.", insight:"Only seventy-four Levites against thousands of others. The servants of God have often been few, but God has never needed many."},
{q:"How many singers, the children of Asaph?", options:["148","184","138","248"], a:0, ref:"Nehemiah 7:44",verse:"The singers: the children of Asaph, an hundred forty and eight.", insight:"Asaph's family carried the music of the temple across the exile. Worship survives captivity when families keep singing."},
{q:"How many porters were appointed?", options:["138","148","183","318"], a:0, ref:"Nehemiah 7:45",verse:"The porters: the children of Shallum, the children of Ater, the children of Talmon, the children of Akkub, the children of Hatita, the children of Shobai, an hundred thirty and eight.", insight:"The gatekeepers are counted with the same care as the singers. In God's house, guarding the door is honorable work."},
{q:"How many were the Nethinims and the children of Solomon's servants together?", options:["392","329","642","293"], a:0, ref:"Nehemiah 7:60",verse:"All the Nethinims, and the children of Solomon’s servants, were three hundred ninety and two.", insight:"The temple servants, once captives and laborers, are numbered among God's people. No one's past disqualifies them from His register."},
{q:"From which places did some come up who could not prove their father's house?", options:["Telmelah, Telharesha, Cherub, Addon, and Immer","Babylon, Susa, and Ecbatana","Bethel, Ai, and Michmas","Lod, Hadid, and Ono"], a:0, ref:"Nehemiah 7:61",verse:"And these were they which went up also from Telmelah, Telharesha, Cherub, Addon, and Immer: but they could not shew their father’s house, nor their seed, whether they were of Israel.", insight:"They made the long journey home but could not prove their lineage. Sincerity is precious, but God still asks for truth about who we are."},
{q:"Which priestly families could not find their register?", options:["Habaiah, Koz, and Barzillai","Jedaiah, Immer, and Pashur","Seraiah, Azariah, and Jeremiah","Eliashib, Joiada, and Jonathan"], a:0, ref:"Nehemiah 7:63",verse:"And of the priests: the children of Habaiah, the children of Koz, the children of Barzillai, which took one of the daughters of Barzillai the Gileadite to wife, and was called after their name.", insight:"Even priests had to show their records. No office, however sacred, exempts a man from proving his calling."},
{q:"What happened to those priests?", options:["They were put from the priesthood as polluted","They were made singers instead","They were sent back to Babylon","They were given the Urim at once"], a:0, ref:"Nehemiah 7:64",verse:"These sought their register among those that were reckoned by genealogy, but it was not found: therefore were they, as polluted, put from the priesthood.", insight:"They were set aside, not as punishment but as protection of what is holy. God guards His priesthood too carefully to admit anyone on his own word."},
{q:"What did the Tirshatha say they must wait for before eating the most holy things?", options:["A priest to stand up with Urim and Thummim","The rebuilding of the temple","A letter from the king","The feast of tabernacles"], a:0, ref:"Nehemiah 7:65",verse:"And the Tirshatha said unto them, that they should not eat of the most holy things, till there stood up a priest with Urim and Thummim.", insight:"The decision was left to God's appointed means, not to human debate. When the answer is unclear, waiting on God is better than rushing ahead."},
{q:"What was the number of the whole congregation together?", options:["42,360","40,000","52,000","36,000"], a:0, ref:"Nehemiah 7:66",verse:"The whole congregation together was forty and two thousand three hundred and threescore,", insight:"Forty-two thousand three hundred and sixty souls, each one counted. God knows His people by number because He knows them by name."},
{q:"How many menservants and maidservants were with them?", options:["7,337","7,373","7,733","6,337"], a:0, ref:"Nehemiah 7:67",verse:"Beside their manservants and their maidservants, of whom there were seven thousand three hundred thirty and seven: and they had two hundred forty and five singing men and singing women.", insight:"The servants are counted too, right alongside the congregation. In God's register, the household staff are not footnotes."},
{q:"How many horses did they have?", options:["736","763","673","376"], a:0, ref:"Nehemiah 7:68",verse:"Their horses, seven hundred thirty and six: their mules, two hundred forty and five:", insight:"Even the livestock is inventoried. Nothing God entrusts to His people is too small to be recorded."},
{q:"What did the Tirshatha himself give to the treasure?", options:["A thousand drams of gold, fifty basons, and five hundred and thirty priests' garments","Two thousand drams of silver","His own house in Jerusalem","Two hundred sheep for sacrifice"], a:0, ref:"Nehemiah 7:70",verse:"And some of the chief of the fathers gave unto the work. The Tirshatha gave to the treasure a thousand drams of gold, fifty basons, five hundred and thirty priests’ garments.", insight:"The governor gave first, and gave generously. Leaders who ask others to give should be first to open their own hands."}
]},
{n:8, name:"The Law Read Aloud", seal:{ref:"Nehemiah 8:10",verse:"Then he said unto them, Go your way, eat the fat, and drink the sweet, and send portions unto them for whom nothing is prepared: for this day is holy unto our Lord: neither be ye sorry; for the joy of the LORD is your strength."}, facts:["Did you know Ezra read from morning until midday, about six hours, while the people stood listening? Their ears were attentive to the book of the law the whole time.","Did you know the Levites did not merely read the law but 'gave the sense' of it, so the people understood? This is the Bible's first picture of preaching that explains, not just recites.","Did you know Israel had not kept the feast of booths with such gladness since the days of Joshua, nearly a thousand years before? Some joys wait centuries to be recovered."], questions:[
{q:"Where did all the people gather as one man?", options:["The street before the water gate","The court of the temple","The king's palace","The valley of Hinnom"], a:0, ref:"Nehemiah 8:1",verse:"And all the people gathered themselves together as one man into the street that was before the water gate; and they spake unto Ezra the scribe to bring the book of the law of Moses, which the LORD had commanded to Israel.", insight:"They met in an open street, not a grand hall. God's word does not need a cathedral; it needs a listening crowd."},
{q:"Whom did the people ask to bring the book of the law of Moses?", options:["Ezra the scribe","Nehemiah the governor","Joshua the priest","Zerubbabel"], a:0, ref:"Nehemiah 8:1",verse:"And all the people gathered themselves together as one man into the street that was before the water gate; and they spake unto Ezra the scribe to bring the book of the law of Moses, which the LORD had commanded to Israel.", insight:"The people themselves asked for the reading. Revival often begins not with a preacher's plan but with a congregation's hunger."},
{q:"On what day did Ezra bring the law before the congregation?", options:["The first day of the seventh month","The tenth day of the seventh month","The first day of the first month","The fourteenth day of Nisan"], a:0, ref:"Nehemiah 8:2",verse:"And Ezra the priest brought the law before the congregation both of men and women, and all that could hear with understanding, upon the first day of the seventh month.", insight:"The seventh month was already sacred with feasts. God timed the reading for a season when hearts were ready to hear."},
{q:"Who stood in the congregation that day?", options:["Men and women, and all that could hear with understanding","Only the priests and Levites","Only men over thirty","The nobles of Judah alone"], a:0, ref:"Nehemiah 8:2",verse:"And Ezra the priest brought the law before the congregation both of men and women, and all that could hear with understanding, upon the first day of the seventh month.", insight:"Everyone who could understand was included, women and all. God's word was never meant for a select few."},
{q:"How long did Ezra read to the people?", options:["From the morning until midday","From evening until morning","For seven days without rest","For a single hour"], a:0, ref:"Nehemiah 8:3",verse:"And he read therein before the street that was before the water gate from the morning until midday, before the men and the women, and those that could understand; and the ears of all the people were attentive unto the book of the law.", insight:"About six hours of reading, and the people stayed. Hunger for God's word can outlast tired feet."},
{q:"What did Ezra stand on to read?", options:["A pulpit of wood made for the purpose","The steps of the temple","A stone altar","The top of the wall"], a:0, ref:"Nehemiah 8:4",verse:"And Ezra the scribe stood upon a pulpit of wood, which they had made for the purpose; and beside him stood Mattithiah, and Shema, and Anaiah, and Urijah, and Hilkiah, and Maaseiah, on his right hand; and on his left hand, Pedaiah, and Mishael, and Malchiah, and Hashum, and Hashbadana, Zechariah, and Meshullam.", insight:"They built a wooden platform just so all could see and hear him. Making God's word visible and audible is worth the carpentry."},
{q:"What did all the people do when Ezra opened the book?", options:["Stood up","Fell on their faces","Covered their heads","Clapped their hands"], a:0, ref:"Nehemiah 8:5",verse:"And Ezra opened the book in the sight of all the people; (for he was above all the people;) and when he opened it, all the people stood up:", insight:"They rose to their feet for the reading, honoring the word with their bodies. Reverence is not just felt; it is shown."},
{q:"How did the people answer when Ezra blessed the LORD?", options:["Amen, Amen, with lifting up their hands","Hosanna in the highest","Holy, holy, holy","Singing a psalm"], a:0, ref:"Nehemiah 8:6",verse:"And Ezra blessed the LORD, the great God. And all the people answered, Amen, Amen, with lifting up their hands: and they bowed their heads, and worshipped the LORD with their faces to the ground.", insight:"Hands lifted, heads bowed, faces to the ground: their bodies preached before the sermon began. True worship involves the whole person."},
{q:"What did the Levites do among the people?", options:["Caused the people to understand the law","Collected the temple tax","Served the showbread","Guarded the gates"], a:0, ref:"Nehemiah 8:7",verse:"Also Jeshua, and Bani, and Sherebiah, Jamin, Akkub, Shabbethai, Hodijah, Maaseiah, Kelita, Azariah, Jozabad, Hanan, Pelaiah, and the Levites, caused the people to understand the law: and the people stood in their place.", insight:"Reading was not enough; someone had to make it plain. God gives teachers so that His word is understood, not just heard."},
{q:"How did they read the book of the law?", options:["Distinctly, and gave the sense, and caused them to understand the reading","In a whisper for the priests alone","In the Persian tongue","As a mournful chant"], a:0, ref:"Nehemiah 8:8",verse:"So they read in the book in the law of God distinctly, and gave the sense, and caused them to understand the reading.", insight:"Distinct reading plus clear explanation: this is the Bible's pattern for preaching. The goal was never performance but understanding."},
{q:"Why did all the people weep?", options:["When they heard the words of the law","Because the wall was broken","Because Ezra rebuked them","Because the feast was cancelled"], a:0, ref:"Nehemiah 8:9",verse:"And Nehemiah, which is the Tirshatha, and Ezra the priest the scribe, and the Levites that taught the people, said unto all the people, This day is holy unto the LORD your God; mourn not, nor weep. For all the people wept, when they heard the words of the law.", insight:"The word cut them before it comforted them. Conviction often comes before joy, and tears before the feast."},
{q:"Who told the people, 'This day is holy unto the LORD your God; mourn not, nor weep'?", options:["Nehemiah the Tirshatha, Ezra the priest the scribe, and the Levites","Sanballat and Tobiah","The king of Persia","The prophets of Jerusalem"], a:0, ref:"Nehemiah 8:9",verse:"And Nehemiah, which is the Tirshatha, and Ezra the priest the scribe, and the Levites that taught the people, said unto all the people, This day is holy unto the LORD your God; mourn not, nor weep. For all the people wept, when they heard the words of the law.", insight:"Their leaders turned weeping into worship. There is a time to mourn over sin, and a time to let God's holiness become your joy."},
{q:"According to this verse, what is the people's strength?", options:["The joy of the LORD","The wall of Jerusalem","The sword of Judah","The king's favor"], a:0, ref:"Nehemiah 8:10",verse:"Then he said unto them, Go your way, eat the fat, and drink the sweet, and send portions unto them for whom nothing is prepared: for this day is holy unto our Lord: neither be ye sorry; for the joy of the LORD is your strength.", insight:"Not their numbers, not their wall, but God's joy. Strength for God's people has always been a gift received, not a muscle flexed."},
{q:"Why did the people go their way with great mirth?", options:["Because they had understood the words declared unto them","Because the wall was finished","Because the king sent gifts","Because the feast lasted seven days"], a:0, ref:"Nehemiah 8:12",verse:"And all the people went their way to eat, and to drink, and to send portions, and to make great mirth, because they had understood the words that were declared unto them.", insight:"Understanding God's word turned their weeping into mirth. Scripture read and understood is a well of joy."},
{q:"On the second day, who gathered to Ezra to understand the words of the law?", options:["The chief of the fathers, the priests, and the Levites","All the children under twelve","The merchants of Jerusalem","The servants of the nobles"], a:0, ref:"Nehemiah 8:13",verse:"And on the second day were gathered together the chief of the fathers of all the people, the priests, and the Levites, unto Ezra the scribe, even to understand the words of the law.", insight:"The leaders came back for more the next day. One hearing was not enough; understanding the word takes repeated, deliberate study."},
{q:"What did they find written about the feast of the seventh month?", options:["That the children of Israel should dwell in booths","That they should fast seven days","That no work be done at all","That the ark be carried around the city"], a:0, ref:"Nehemiah 8:14",verse:"And they found written in the law which the LORD had commanded by Moses, that the children of Israel should dwell in booths in the feast of the seventh month:", insight:"Study led straight to obedience. They found it written, and they did it; the shortest distance between the Bible and life is action."},
{q:"What branches were they to fetch for the booths?", options:["Olive, pine, myrtle, palm, and branches of thick trees","Cedar, cypress, and fir","Oak, ash, and elm","Willow, poplar, and birch"], a:0, ref:"Nehemiah 8:15",verse:"And that they should publish and proclaim in all their cities, and in Jerusalem, saying, Go forth unto the mount, and fetch olive branches, and pine branches, and myrtle branches, and palm branches, and branches of thick trees, to make booths, as it is written.", insight:"God specified the branches, down to the myrtle and palm. Obedience pays attention to details; love does not skim the instructions."},
{q:"Since whose days had Israel not kept the feast in this way?", options:["Jeshua the son of Nun","David the king","Solomon","Moses"], a:0, ref:"Nehemiah 8:17",verse:"And all the congregation of them that were come again out of the captivity made booths, and sat under the booths: for since the days of Jeshua the son of Nun unto that day had not the children of Israel done so. And there was very great gladness.", insight:"Nearly a thousand years had passed since Joshua's day. Some joys wait centuries to be recovered, but they are worth the wait."},
{q:"What filled that feast?", options:["Very great gladness","Deep mourning","Solemn silence","Bitter weeping"], a:0, ref:"Nehemiah 8:17",verse:"And all the congregation of them that were come again out of the captivity made booths, and sat under the booths: for since the days of Jeshua the son of Nun unto that day had not the children of Israel done so. And there was very great gladness.", insight:"The chapter that began with weeping ends with very great gladness. God's word leads mourners to merriment when it is understood and obeyed."},
{q:"How long did they keep the feast, and what came on the eighth day?", options:["Seven days, and on the eighth day a solemn assembly","Three days, then a fast","Forty days, then a jubile","One day only"], a:0, ref:"Nehemiah 8:18",verse:"Also day by day, from the first day unto the last day, he read in the book of the law of God. And they kept the feast seven days; and on the eighth day was a solemn assembly, according unto the manner.", insight:"Seven days of booths and an eighth day of assembly: they followed the manner exactly as written. Joy and order are not enemies in God's worship."}
]},
{n:9, name:"A Prayer of Confession", seal:{ref:"Nehemiah 9:17",verse:"And refused to obey, neither were mindful of thy wonders that thou didst among them; but hardened their necks, and in their rebellion appointed a captain to return to their bondage: but thou art a God ready to pardon, gracious and merciful, slow to anger, and of great kindness, and forsookest them not."}, facts:["Did you know this single prayer walks through nearly a thousand years, from Abraham to the return from exile? Confession here means telling the whole story honestly, not skipping the shameful chapters.","Did you know the prayer names the pattern out loud: God blessed, Israel rebelled, God disciplined, Israel cried out, and God rescued, again and again? Naming the cycle is the first step to breaking it.","Did you know the chapter ends with a written covenant, sealed by the princes, Levites, and priests? Their prayer did not end with feelings; it ended with signatures."], questions:[
{q:"On which day of the month did the children of Israel assemble with fasting?", options:["The twenty-fourth day","The first day","The tenth day","The fifteenth day"], a:0, ref:"Nehemiah 9:1",verse:"Now in the twenty and fourth day of this month the children of Israel were assembled with fasting, and with sackclothes, and earth upon them.", insight:"They chose a day and showed up in sackcloth. Confession works best when it is scheduled, not just felt."},
{q:"What did the seed of Israel do as the assembly began?", options:["Separated themselves from all strangers, and stood and confessed their sins","Offered a thousand burnt offerings","Built a new altar","Marched around the city"], a:0, ref:"Nehemiah 9:2",verse:"And the seed of Israel separated themselves from all strangers, and stood and confessed their sins, and the iniquities of their fathers.", insight:"Before the prayer came separation and confession. You cannot hold onto the world with one hand and cling to God with the other."},
{q:"How did they spend the day?", options:["One fourth reading the law, another fourth confessing and worshipping","Half feasting and half fasting","The whole day in silence","Morning prayer and evening sacrifice"], a:0, ref:"Nehemiah 9:3",verse:"And they stood up in their place, and read in the book of the law of the LORD their God one fourth part of the day; and another fourth part they confessed, and worshipped the LORD their God.", insight:"Half the day in the word, half in confession and worship. They gave God their hours, not just their feelings."},
{q:"What did the Levites call the people to do?", options:["Stand up and bless the LORD your God for ever and ever","Sit down and be silent","Go home to your houses","Bring gifts to the temple"], a:0, ref:"Nehemiah 9:5",verse:"Then the Levites, Jeshua, and Kadmiel, Bani, Hashabniah, Sherebiah, Hodijah, Shebaniah, and Pethahiah, said, Stand up and bless the LORD your God for ever and ever: and blessed be thy glorious name, which is exalted above all blessing and praise.", insight:"'Stand up and bless' came before the long prayer. Worship postures the heart; you rise to bless before you kneel to confess."},
{q:"Whom did God choose and bring out of Ur of the Chaldees?", options:["Abram, and gave him the name Abraham","Moses, and called him Israel","David, and made him king","Jacob, and named him Judah"], a:0, ref:"Nehemiah 9:7",verse:"Thou art the LORD the God, who didst choose Abram, and broughtest him forth out of Ur of the Chaldees, and gavest him the name of Abraham;", insight:"The prayer begins with grace: God chose Abram before Abram did anything. Every story of salvation starts with God choosing first."},
{q:"Where did God see their fathers' affliction and hear their cry?", options:["In Egypt, and by the Red sea","In Babylon, and by the river Chebar","In Assyria, and at Mount Carmel","In the wilderness, and at Sinai"], a:0, ref:"Nehemiah 9:9",verse:"And didst see the affliction of our fathers in Egypt, and heardest their cry by the Red sea;", insight:"God both saw the affliction and heard the cry. Your suffering is never unseen, and your prayer is never unheard."},
{q:"What became of their persecutors at the sea?", options:["They were thrown into the deeps as a stone into the mighty waters","They turned back at the shore","They were blinded by the pillar","They drowned in the river"], a:0, ref:"Nehemiah 9:11",verse:"And thou didst divide the sea before them, so that they went through the midst of the sea on the dry land; and their persecutors thou threwest into the deeps, as a stone into the mighty waters.", insight:"Like a stone in mighty waters: swift, total, and final. When God deals with what pursues you, He does not do it halfway."},
{q:"How did God lead them day and night?", options:["By a cloudy pillar in the day and a pillar of fire in the night","By the stars alone","By Moses' rod","By a map drawn in the sand"], a:0, ref:"Nehemiah 9:12",verse:"Moreover thou leddest them in the day by a cloudy pillar; and in the night by a pillar of fire, to give them light in the way wherein they should go.", insight:"Cloud by day, fire by night: guidance you could see. God still leads His people visibly enough to follow, one day at a time."},
{q:"On which mountain did God come down and speak from heaven?", options:["Mount Sinai","Mount Zion","Mount Carmel","Mount Nebo"], a:0, ref:"Nehemiah 9:13",verse:"Thou camest down also upon mount Sinai, and spakest with them from heaven, and gavest them right judgments, and true laws, good statutes and commandments:", insight:"At Sinai God spoke from heaven and gave 'true laws, good statutes.' His commands are not burdens; the prayer calls them good."},
{q:"What bread and water did God give them in the wilderness?", options:["Bread from heaven, and water out of the rock","Corn from Egypt, and water from the Nile","Fish from the sea, and dew from heaven","Fruit from Canaan, and water from wells"], a:0, ref:"Nehemiah 9:15",verse:"And gavest them bread from heaven for their hunger, and broughtest forth water for them out of the rock for their thirst, and promisedst them that they should go in to possess the land which thou hadst sworn to give them.", insight:"Heaven's bread for hunger, rock's water for thirst. God meets the exact need, in the exact place of lack."},
{q:"How does the prayer describe God at its very heart?", options:["A God ready to pardon, gracious and merciful, slow to anger","A God who forgets His promises","A God who delights in judgment","A distant God, hard to find"], a:0, ref:"Nehemiah 9:17",verse:"And refused to obey, neither were mindful of thy wonders that thou didst among them; but hardened their necks, and in their rebellion appointed a captain to return to their bondage: but thou art a God ready to pardon, gracious and merciful, slow to anger, and of great kindness, and forsookest them not.", insight:"In the middle of a confession of rebellion, the prayer stops to praise a pardoning God. The worse the sin looks, the brighter His mercy shines."},
{q:"What did the people make even after all God's wonders?", options:["A molten calf","A golden crown","A bronze serpent","An iron altar"], a:0, ref:"Nehemiah 9:18",verse:"Yea, when they had made them a molten calf, and said, This is thy God that brought thee up out of Egypt, and had wrought great provocations;", insight:"Fresh from the Red Sea, they cast a calf and called it their god. The prayer does not hide this shame; honest confession names the worst of it."},
{q:"For how many years did God sustain them, and what two wonders marked those years?", options:["Forty years; their clothes waxed not old, and their feet swelled not","Seventy years; they never hungered or thirsted","Twelve years; the manna never ceased","One hundred years; none of them died"], a:0, ref:"Nehemiah 9:21",verse:"Yea, forty years didst thou sustain them in the wilderness, so that they lacked nothing; their clothes waxed not old, and their feet swelled not.", insight:"Forty years, and not a worn-out garment or a swollen foot. God's provision covers the details you never think to pray about."},
{q:"Whose lands did they possess?", options:["Sihon, the king of Heshbon, and Og king of Bashan","Pharaoh and the Philistines","Nebuchadnezzar and Cyrus","The Hittites and Jebusites only"], a:0, ref:"Nehemiah 9:22",verse:"Moreover thou gavest them kingdoms and nations, and didst divide them into corners: so they possessed the land of Sihon, and the land of the king of Heshbon, and the land of Og king of Bashan.", insight:"Kingdoms fell because God gave them. The victories of God's people are gifts received, not trophies earned."},
{q:"What did they find in the good land?", options:["Strong cities, a fat land, houses full of goods, wells digged, vineyards and oliveyards","Empty deserts and dry wells","Only tents and flocks","Cities of gold and silver"], a:0, ref:"Nehemiah 9:25",verse:"And they took strong cities, and a fat land, and possessed houses full of all goods, wells digged, vineyards, and oliveyards, and fruit trees in abundance: so they did eat, and were filled, and became fat, and delighted themselves in thy great goodness.", insight:"They inherited houses they did not build and wells they did not dig. Grace often looks like abundance you did not earn."},
{q:"What did they do to the prophets God sent to turn them back?", options:["Slew them","Crowned them kings","Built them houses","Sent them to Babylon"], a:0, ref:"Nehemiah 9:26",verse:"Nevertheless they were disobedient, and rebelled against thee, and cast thy law behind their backs, and slew thy prophets which testified against them to turn them to thee, and they wrought great provocations.", insight:"They killed the very men sent to save them. The prayer admits it plainly, because confession that edits the story is not confession."},
{q:"When they cried to God in their trouble, what did He give them?", options:["Saviours, who saved them out of the hand of their enemies","Greater affliction","A new king","Silence"], a:0, ref:"Nehemiah 9:27",verse:"Therefore thou deliveredst them into the hand of their enemies, who vexed them: and in the time of their trouble, when they cried unto thee, thou heardest them from heaven; and according to thy manifold mercies thou gavest them saviours, who saved them out of the hand of their enemies.", insight:"Every cry was answered with a saviour. No matter how many times they fell, God's mercy kept sending rescue."},
{q:"What did Israel do after they had rest?", options:["They did evil again before thee","They built the temple","They kept the sabbath","They returned to Egypt"], a:0, ref:"Nehemiah 9:28",verse:"But after they had rest, they did evil again before thee: therefore leftest thou them in the hand of their enemies, so that they had the dominion over them: yet when they returned, and cried unto thee, thou heardest them from heaven; and many times didst thou deliver them according to thy mercies;", insight:"Rest became the doorway back to rebellion. The prayer names the cycle honestly: blessing, forgetting, falling, crying, rescue, repeat."},
{q:"How does the prayer sum up God's justice and their sin?", options:["Thou hast done right, but we have done wickedly","God was harsh and they were innocent","Both sides were at fault","God forgot and they remembered"], a:0, ref:"Nehemiah 9:33",verse:"Howbeit thou art just in all that is brought upon us; for thou hast done right, but we have done wickedly:", insight:"This is the whole prayer in one line: God right, we wrong. True confession never shares the blame."},
{q:"What did they do because of all this?", options:["Made a sure covenant, wrote it, and had it sealed by their princes, Levites, and priests","Built a monument of stones","Offered a thousand sacrifices","Appointed a new king"], a:0, ref:"Nehemiah 9:38",verse:"And because of all this we make a sure covenant, and write it; and our princes, Levites, and priests, seal unto it.", insight:"Prayer ended in signatures. Real repentance does not stop at feeling sorry; it writes down the promise and seals it."}
]},
{n:10, name:"The Sealed Covenant", seal:{ref:"Nehemiah 10:39",verse:"For the children of Israel and the children of Levi shall bring the offering of the corn, of the new wine, and the oil, unto the chambers, where are the vessels of the sanctuary, and the priests that minister, and the porters, and the singers: and we will not forsake the house of our God."}, facts:["Did you know the covenant was a written document, sealed by the governor, priests, Levites, and family heads? In Nehemiah's day a seal was a man's mark of honor; they staked their names on obedience.","Did you know every person charged himself a yearly temple tax of a third of a shekel? Worship had a budget, and everyone paid into it, not just the wealthy.","Did you know they cast lots to decide whose turn it was to supply wood for the altar? Nothing in God's house was left to chance; every family took its turn."], questions:[
{q:"Who are named first among those that sealed the covenant?", options:["Nehemiah the Tirshatha, the son of Hachaliah, and Zidkijah","Ezra the scribe and Zerubbabel","Sanballat and Tobiah","The twelve Levites"], a:0, ref:"Nehemiah 10:1",verse:"Now those that sealed were, Nehemiah, the Tirshatha, the son of Hachaliah, and Zidkijah,", insight:"The governor signed first. When leaders put their names down first, the people follow with confidence."},
{q:"Which priests head the list of those who sealed?", options:["Seraiah, Azariah, Jeremiah","Jeshua, Bani, and Kadmiel","Parosh, Pahath-moab, and Elam","Hattush, Shebaniah, and Malluch"], a:0, ref:"Nehemiah 10:2",verse:"Seraiah, Azariah, Jeremiah,", insight:"The priests sealed, and their names are recorded. God remembers those who publicly commit themselves to Him."},
{q:"Which Levites are named as sealers?", options:["Jeshua the son of Azaniah, Binnui of the sons of Henadad, and Kadmiel","Asaph, Heman, and Jeduthun","Eliashib, Joiada, and Jonathan","Mattithiah, Shema, and Anaiah"], a:0, ref:"Nehemiah 10:9",verse:"And the Levites: both Jeshua the son of Azaniah, Binnui of the sons of Henadad, Kadmiel;", insight:"The Levites who had taught the law now signed it. Those who teach God's word should be first to bind themselves to it."},
{q:"Which names head the chief of the people who sealed?", options:["Parosh, Pahath-moab, Elam, Zatthu, Bani","Telmelah, Telharesha, and Cherub","Jedaiah, Immer, and Pashur","Senaah, Harim, and Jericho"], a:0, ref:"Nehemiah 10:14",verse:"The chief of the people; Parosh, Pahath-moab, Elam, Zatthu, Bani,", insight:"Family heads signed for their houses. Covenant faithfulness starts at home, with the head of the house leading."},
{q:"Who else joined the covenant?", options:["The rest of the people, and all who had separated themselves from the peoples of the lands unto the law of God","Only the nobles","The Samaritans","The king's officers"], a:0, ref:"Nehemiah 10:28",verse:"And the rest of the people, the priests, the Levites, the porters, the singers, the Nethinims, and all they that had separated themselves from the people of the lands unto the law of God, their wives, their sons, and their daughters, every one having knowledge, and having understanding;", insight:"Everyone with understanding joined, from porters to Nethinims. God's covenant has room for all who separate themselves to Him."},
{q:"What did they enter into?", options:["A curse and an oath, to walk in God's law","A trade agreement with Tyre","A treaty with Persia","A vow of silence"], a:0, ref:"Nehemiah 10:29",verse:"They clave to their brethren, their nobles, and entered into a curse, and into an oath, to walk in God’s law, which was given by Moses the servant of God, and to observe and do all the commandments of the LORD our Lord, and his judgments and his statutes;", insight:"They bound themselves with a curse and an oath, serious words for a serious promise. Covenant with God is not casual; it is sworn."},
{q:"What did they pledge about marriage?", options:["Not to give their daughters to the peoples of the land, nor take their daughters for their sons","To marry only Levites","To pay a dowry of silver","To wed in the seventh month"], a:0, ref:"Nehemiah 10:30",verse:"And that we would not give our daughters unto the people of the land, nor take their daughters for our sons:", insight:"They guarded the next generation at the altar. Who you marry shapes who your children will worship."},
{q:"What did they pledge about the sabbath?", options:["Not to buy ware or victuals on the sabbath day or the holy day","To work twice as hard","To keep the gates open","To sell only to strangers"], a:0, ref:"Nehemiah 10:31",verse:"And if the people of the land bring ware or any victuals on the sabbath day to sell, that we would not buy it of them on the sabbath, or on the holy day: and that we would leave the seventh year, and the exaction of every debt.", insight:"They closed their wallets one day in seven. Keeping the sabbath cost them trade, which is exactly what made it a sacrifice."},
{q:"What did they pledge about the seventh year?", options:["To leave it, and the exaction of every debt","To double the harvest","To rebuild the wall","To crown a new king"], a:0, ref:"Nehemiah 10:31",verse:"And if the people of the land bring ware or any victuals on the sabbath day to sell, that we would not buy it of them on the sabbath, or on the holy day: and that we would leave the seventh year, and the exaction of every debt.", insight:"Every seventh year the land rested and debts were released. God's people were to let both the soil and the debtor breathe."},
{q:"What yearly charge did they lay on themselves for the house of God?", options:["The third part of a shekel","A tenth of all their flocks","A thousand drams of gold","Fifty basons of silver"], a:0, ref:"Nehemiah 10:32",verse:"Also we made ordinances for us, to charge ourselves yearly with the third part of a shekel for the service of the house of our God;", insight:"Worship had a budget, and everyone paid into it. A third of a shekel a year kept the temple's lamps lit."},
{q:"What was this charge meant to cover?", options:["The shewbread, the continual offerings, the sabbaths, new moons, set feasts, and sin offerings","The building of the wall","The king's tribute","The army's wages"], a:0, ref:"Nehemiah 10:33",verse:"For the shewbread, and for the continual meat offering, and for the continual burnt offering, of the sabbaths, of the new moons, for the set feasts, and for the holy things, and for the sin offerings to make an atonement for Israel, and for all the work of the house of our God.", insight:"They funded the daily bread, the daily offerings, and the atonement for Israel. Giving kept the worship going and the sins covered."},
{q:"For what did they cast lots?", options:["The wood offering, to bring it into the house of God","The choice of a new governor","The division of the land","The order of the singers"], a:0, ref:"Nehemiah 10:34",verse:"And we cast the lots among the priests, the Levites, and the people, for the wood offering, to bring it into the house of our God, after the houses of our fathers, at times appointed year by year, to burn upon the altar of the LORD our God, as it is written in the law:", insight:"They cast lots for who would supply the altar's wood. Even the firewood was assigned fairly; no holy task was beneath the lot."},
{q:"How often was the wood to be brought?", options:["Year by year, at times appointed","Only once","Every sabbath","Every seventh year"], a:0, ref:"Nehemiah 10:34",verse:"And we cast the lots among the priests, the Levites, and the people, for the wood offering, to bring it into the house of our God, after the houses of our fathers, at times appointed year by year, to burn upon the altar of the LORD our God, as it is written in the law:", insight:"Year by year, at appointed times: steady, scheduled giving. Faithfulness is mostly a calendar, not a feeling."},
{q:"What firstfruits were they to bring year by year?", options:["The firstfruits of the ground and of all fruit of all trees","Only the firstborn sons","Gold from Ophir","Spices from Sheba"], a:0, ref:"Nehemiah 10:35",verse:"And to bring the firstfruits of our ground, and the firstfruits of all fruit of all trees, year by year, unto the house of the LORD:", insight:"The first of the harvest went to God's house before any went to their own. Firstfruits declare who is first in your life."},
{q:"What else were they to bring to the house of God?", options:["The firstborn of their sons and of their cattle, and the firstlings of herds and flocks","Their old garments","Weapons of war","Foreign idols"], a:0, ref:"Nehemiah 10:36",verse:"Also the firstborn of our sons, and of our cattle, as it is written in the law, and the firstlings of our herds and of our flocks, to bring to the house of our God, unto the priests that minister in the house of our God:", insight:"The firstborn belonged to God, a living reminder that every life is His gift. Dedicating the first consecrates the rest."},
{q:"To whom were the tithes of the ground to go?", options:["To the Levites","To the king","To the merchants","To the poor of Samaria"], a:0, ref:"Nehemiah 10:37",verse:"And that we should bring the firstfruits of our dough, and our offerings, and the fruit of all manner of trees, of wine and of oil, unto the priests, to the chambers of the house of our God; and the tithes of our ground unto the Levites, that the same Levites might have the tithes in all the cities of our tillage.", insight:"The Levites had no land of their own, so the tithe was their portion. God provides for His servants through His people's obedience."},
{q:"What were they to bring to the priests, to the chambers of the house of God?", options:["The firstfruits of dough, offerings, fruit, wine, and oil","Swords and shields","Timber and stone","Horses and chariots"], a:0, ref:"Nehemiah 10:37",verse:"And that we should bring the firstfruits of our dough, and our offerings, and the fruit of all manner of trees, of wine and of oil, unto the priests, to the chambers of the house of our God; and the tithes of our ground unto the Levites, that the same Levites might have the tithes in all the cities of our tillage.", insight:"Dough, wine, and oil: the staples of every table went first to God's house. Ordinary provisions become holy when given first."},
{q:"Who was to be with the Levites when they took tithes?", options:["The priest the son of Aaron","The Tirshatha","The king's scribe","The chief singer"], a:0, ref:"Nehemiah 10:38",verse:"And the priest the son of Aaron shall be with the Levites, when the Levites take tithes: and the Levites shall bring up the tithe of the tithes unto the house of our God, to the chambers, into the treasure house.", insight:"A priest oversaw the Levites' tithing, keeping even the collectors accountable. In God's house, everybody answers to somebody."},
{q:"What were the Levites to bring up to the house of God?", options:["The tithe of the tithes, to the chambers, into the treasure house","The ark of the covenant","The tables of the law","The golden calf"], a:0, ref:"Nehemiah 10:38",verse:"And the priest the son of Aaron shall be with the Levites, when the Levites take tithes: and the Levites shall bring up the tithe of the tithes unto the house of our God, to the chambers, into the treasure house.", insight:"The Levites tithed on their tithes. Nobody is exempt from giving, not even those who live by the gifts of others."},
{q:"What is the closing pledge of the chapter?", options:["We will not forsake the house of our God","We will build a new temple","We will return to Babylon","We will make Nehemiah king"], a:0, ref:"Nehemiah 10:39",verse:"For the children of Israel and the children of Levi shall bring the offering of the corn, of the new wine, and the oil, unto the chambers, where are the vessels of the sanctuary, and the priests that minister, and the porters, and the singers: and we will not forsake the house of our God.", insight:"The covenant ends where it must: 'we will not forsake the house of our God.' Every promise in the chapter funnels into this one resolve."}
]}
]);
